summary:
Call a method in another object. Because the return value is that of the called method, its type cannot be determined at compile time; checking the type when using it is advisable. The string `func` names the method to call in object `ob`. Arguments 3, 4, and onward are passed to `func` as arguments 1, 2, and onward, in order.

   If the first object argument to call_other is an array, call_other invokes `func` on each object and also returns an array.

   If the second argument to call_other is an array, its first element must be the method-name string `func`; the remaining elements are the arguments passed to that method.

   A more convenient way to use call_other() is object->function_name(arg1, arg2). This syntax is automatically converted into the equivalent call_other(object, "function_name", arg1, arg2) invocation.

   object ob | object *obs -> func ( ... );

   For example:
   call_other(ob, "query", "name");

   can be written as:
   ob->query("name");

   Using an array as the second argument, the example can be written as:
   call_other(ob, ({ "query", "name" }));

   An example with an array as the first argument is:
   users()->quit();
